<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Marcomanni</h1><p class="st-meta">58 BCE – 430 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Suebian Germanic people who settled Bohemia and fought Rome in the Marcomannic Wars, the hardest frontier struggle of Marcus Aurelius' reign.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Marcomanni ('border men') first appear among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariovistus">Ariovistus</a>' followers in 58 BCE, probably living near the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and Main. Around 9 BCE, under pressure from Roman campaigns led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero-claudius-drusus">Drusus</a>, their leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maroboduus">Maroboduus</a> led them east into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Bohemia</a>, the former land of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/boii">Boii</a>, where they formed the core of a powerful kingdom.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maroboduus">Maroboduus</a> fell, the Marcomanni were usually ruled by kings who acted as Roman clients, receiving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a> and trading through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnuntum">Carnuntum</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. Roman goods are common in their graves. From 166/167 they and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/quadi">Quadi</a> broke through the frontier, sparking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcomannic-wars">Marcomannic Wars</a>; around 170 they besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a> fought them until his death in 180.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commodus">Commodus</a> made peace in 180, and the Marcomanni remained a frontier people for two more centuries, with a queen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fritigil">Fritigil</a>, converting to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> around 396. They faded from the record in the 5th century, many joining the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> or merging into the people later called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavarians">Bavarians</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/marcomanni.jpg" alt="Marcomanni" width="320" height="159"><figcaption>Image: Andrew Lancaster · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Suebi.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>58 BCE – 430 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Maroboduus, Ballomar, Fritigil</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Germanic paganism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Elbe Germanic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>, <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>, <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Marcomannic confederation</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=186&amp;focus=marcomanni" aria-label="Marcomanni on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5033 434 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Marcomanni lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5353 690h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5353 690h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Czech Republic. <a href="/atlas?year=186&amp;focus=marcomanni">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
